 clearly this  country needs  you've heard me say again and again when  we talk about  building the infrastructure and creating  jobs in our country we have to think big  and transformatively  as thomas jefferson did with his first  initiative  in this regard as teddy roosevelt did  with the national park service that he  created 100 years  anniversary of what thomas jefferson did  what president eisenhower did  in the interstate highway system that he  established  in the interest of our nation's security  and now with president biden is  presenting  all-american transformative thinking  big built back better  and as we say in the house in every zip  code  in america so that everyone  has a full advantage of what the  infrastructure does it creates jobs in  the building  it creates commerce in the moving of  products who from farm to produce from  farm to  market or other products from production  to the marketplace so people to school  and work and the rest  clean air for our children clean water  for them to drink get the lead  out again and again to talk about  how we do this in a way that involves  everyone in our country  all across our country we hope to do  this in the most bipartisan way possible  infrastructure has not been a partisan  issue  in the past we hope that it would not be  now  and so again we  we don't want to do this in a way that  is ancient we wanted to do it as it is  the future  so many times you've heard people say we  want to be on the right side of  of history no we want to be on the right  side of history out but we want to be on  the right side  of the future as well so it's the  building infrastructure is a safety  measure as well the safety of our  bridges our roads  safety of the water our children drink  the list goes on  and then when we return we have a  legislative field weekly for when we  went  broke for the holy season uh we did have  a time for committee work that has  prepared us to bring legislation to the  floor  when we come back uh in this next week  but then i'd be pleased to take any  questions you may have  if you'd like to be added to the queue  to ask a question please select the  raise hand option under the reactions  tab at the bottom of your screen  one moment while we wait for reporters  to join the queue  thank you drew  while we're waiting for reporters i'm  excited about saturday but i'll be  joining the national domestic workers  alliance  is the director and in case you don't  know her she's an iconic leader  for caregivers in our country really  heroin and i'm so honored to be invited  to join  their initiative on saturday any  questions  our first question comes from eric  wassen with bloomberg news  hi speaker thanks so much for doing this  if you could tell us a little bit about  what are the next steps on this american  jobs  plan uh getting it together what  decisions you're gonna be making  and some confusion about this american  families plan is it possible  that gets wrapped into the jobs plan and  how do you see approaching that issue  thank you  thank you well it's pretty exciting uh  when we're talking about this  this next bill a few weeks ago i  uh suggested or tasked or  called upon my committee chairs  to reach across the aisle on the the  infrastructure aspects of this bill  and to see where we can find  bipartisanship because as i said this  has never been partisan  but when we go forward with this  legislation we have to  involve everyone and that means women  who are in the world  who should be participating in how we  build uh  the future of our country as well and  for that to happen  uh what was in the uh in our previous  bill in the rescue package and what will  be in  the family bill are very important when  we talk about child care elder care and  the rest  so everyone can participate so these  things are related if  if you're going to build the  infrastructure you have to have  workforce development  education is very very important in that  when we talk about  investing in education we recognize that  nothing brings more money to the  treasury  nothing than education of the american  people early childhood k-12  higher education post-grad lifetime  learning  training for our lifetime training for  our workers  so that they can build for the future so  all of these things  are related and and um uh i don't  i i think we will have two bills that we  will have this uh bill but  we're hoping that we can do especially  the infrastructure bill  where as i said now for the third time  it's always been bipartisan or  nonpartisan  uh that we will do so in a partisan in a  bipartisan way  if we have to go to reconciliation  that's a lever but i hope it's not  something that we need to do  and con the time wise uh mr mr defazio  thinks that we can  do our part in the house probably in the  month of  may at least his committee would be  ready  at that time i would hope that our part  in the house would be  largely done before the fourth of july  whether the whole package can be done  then  we just don't know but as some have  suggested we want to do it before the  august break  now our plan does have not only  infrastructure  roads mass transit water broadband  education housing it has a number  the definition of infrastructure in a  broader sense  but also how how we do it what we do  and how we do it in a way that involves  jobs  think of this as the american american  jobs plan  as we just finished the american rescue  plan  and our next question comes from lindsay  mcpherson with roll call  um related to what you said about  wanting  the bills to be bipartisan how do you  judge that  is it when the bills go to markup  whether they get any bipartisan support  is it before markup  when do you expect to make the  determination on whether it's possible  to do it in a very personal way  lindsay oh thank you i uh we want  bipartisanship every step of the way  and again we want to build consensus  within our own party  as we go forward and again many of the  projects that we're talking about  have regional significance and they are  bipartisan  in their region so we were hoping that  we can translate  the needs of the communities across the  country  which are nonpartisan into a  legislation that works for for everyone  uh  so every step of the way you know  whether it's in uh like  for example they had a committee hearing  on  electrification before the  break for holy week etc and mr  defazio presided as as chair of the  committee  mr graves there also another and  bipartisan  uh hearing the  some of one of the people testified and  i've said this i think last week in our  meeting  the head of um  fedex he said we're all we're going  electric we're going electric we think  now i'm staying separate them that we  think that the postal system will go  electric  many uh fleets will be going electric so  we need to have  the infrastructure for  recharging around the country the  distribution  of of those charging stations is  absolutely essential  uh to what we want to do to be  competitive in the world with our cars  so it's  it's about jobs in every way  and it's also about having the  infrastructure to  support the new  automobiles that we want to be  competitive and selling in the rest of  the world so that was a positive meeting  i  i didn't hear too much complaint from  the republicans  coming out of that and much of what  we want to do is going to be based on  data  which hearings will provide for us  so again based on information  facts data science  science science i think that  in the communities we'll see more common  ground than we have seen  in the congress that was true in the  rescue package  uh i hope that it is a more bipartisan  as we go into the infrastructure but  every every step of the way  and again as we were we were receiving  a briefing from secretary of the  treasury the other day  let's we're here to listen she said  we're here to listen to people's 